Most of the Flow Operations are the same as the Collection.
map: transforms. Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: varriable.map{operation}
filter: filters according to the given conditions.  Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: varriable.filter{Condition}
take: Take the given nummber of elements.  Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: variable.take(number)
drop: Ignore the number of element and take the rest.  Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: variable.drop()
merge: merges two given flow.  Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: merge(flow1,flow2)
zip: merges but we need to give the format it is merged in. Flow stops opperation when one of the flow empties.
Syntax:flow1.zip(flow2) { f1, f2 -> "${f1}_${f2}" }
combine: same as flow but thhis doesn't end when one is finsih and uses the last element of the finished flow to complete opperation.
Syntax:flow1.combine(flow2) { f1, f2 -> "${f1}_${f2}" }
fold: Same as Collection Processing Counter Part.
Syntax: variable.fold(starting) { acc, i -> acc + i }
scan: Same as fold but provides the result after each step
Syntax: variable.scan(starting) { acc, i -> acc + i }
flatMapConcat: flatMapConcat takes each value from a Flow, creates another Flow from it, and processes those produced flows one after another. So, the next Flow starts only after the previous Flow finishes.
Example:
fun flowFrom(elem: String) = flowOf(1, 2, 3)
    .onEach { delay(1000) }
    .map { "${it}_$elem" }
suspend fun main() {
    flowOf("A", "B", "C")
        .flatMapConcat { flowFrom(it) }
        .collect { println(it) }
}
Output:
1_A
2_A
3_A
1_B
2_B
3_B
1_C
2_C
3_C
flatMapMerge:flatMapMerge processes the produced Flows concurrently.
Example:
flowOf("A", "B", "C")
    .flatMapMerge { flowFrom(it) }
    .collect { println(it) }
Output:
1_A
1_B
1_C
2_A
2_B
2_C
3_A
3_B
3_C
You can control how many inner Flows run at the same time:
.flatMapMerge(concurrency = 2) {
    flowFrom(it)
}
The default concurrency is 16.Useful when you want to perform requests for many Flow elements while limiting how many requests happen simultaneously.
flatMapLatest: flatMapLatest keeps the latest Flow. When a new value arrives, the previous Flow is cancelled/forgotten and the new Flow becomes active.
Example:
flowOf("A", "B", "C")
    .flatMapLatest { flowFrom(it) }
    .collect { println(it) }
The latest value "C" gets the final Flow processing. You usually don't want old searches to finish. You want only the latest search.
retry: retry restarts a Flow when an exception occurs.
flow
    .retry(...)
Retry is implemented using retryWhen.
Syntax
.retry(retries = 3) {
    true
}
retryWhen:retryWhen gives more control over retrying. It receives cause and attempt.
Example:
.retryWhen { cause, attempt ->
    println("Attempt: $attempt")
    true
}
distinctUntilChanged:distinctUntilChanged removes consecutive duplicate values.
Example:
flowOf(1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 1, 1, 3)
    .distinctUntilChanged()
    .collect { print(it) }
Output:
123213
Only duplicates that are next to each other are removed. It does not remove every duplicate.
distinctUntilChangedBy: Use this when you want to compare values using a specific property.
Syntax:
users
    .distinctUntilChangedBy { it.id }
Example:
data class User(
    val id: Int,
    val name: String
)
users.distinctUntilChangedBy { it.id }
Now consecutive users with the same id are treated as duplicates.
distinctUntilChanged with Custom Comparison: You can also provide your own comparison.
Syntax:
.distinctUntilChanged { previous, next ->
    previous.id == next.id ||
    previous.name == next.name
}
The lambda decides whether the previous and next values should be treated as equal.
collect:collect is the most common terminal operation used to consume a Flow.
Syntax:
flow.collect {
    println(it)
}
Example:
flowOf(1, 2, 3)
    .collect {
        println(it)
    }
Output:
1
2
3
Terminal operations are suspending functions and finish when the Flow completes.
count:Counts the number of values emitted by the Flow.
Syntax:
val count = flow.count()
Example:
val flow = flowOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
println(flow.count())
Output:
4
first:Returns the first emitted value.
val first = flow.first()
Example:
flowOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
    .first()
Output:
1
firstOrNull: Similar to first, but can return null when there is no value.
Syntax:
val value = flow.firstOrNull()